## I use AI every day. That's why I keep hiring people.

I use AI every single working day. And it has made me more committed than ever to hiring human beings.

That sounds backwards, I know. The story our industry keeps telling goes the other way. AI writes the ads now. AI makes the images. Pretty soon you won't need an agency at all, just a subscription and a decent prompt.

I play guitar, badly enough that my kids have opinions about it. Here's what music taught me about this. A drum machine keeps perfect time. It never rushes the chorus, never shows up late, never asks for a raise. And nobody has ever walked out of a concert humming the drum machine.

People remember the moment a human did something nobody saw coming. The bend in the note. The line that made the whole room laugh. That's the thing a machine can't fake, and it's the thing that sells.

So at Revvia we don't treat AI as a replacement for people. We treat it as the tool that lets our people spend more of their time doing what only people can do. I call that the marketing superpower: human creativity, with AI carrying the bags.

## We hire human whenever we can

Our rule is simple. If a person can do the job, we hire a person.

The first reason is the work itself. Humans bring a kind of creativity AI can't reach. AI is built to predict the most likely next word, the most likely image, the most likely idea. Great marketing is almost never the most likely idea. It's the one that makes a founder lean back and say, "Huh. I never would have thought of that."

The second reason is bigger than marketing. I believe it's better for all of us when businesses hire people as often as they can. A paycheck here in North County puts food on a table, keeps a kid in soccer, and keeps a neighborhood alive. I don't want to build a company that quietly trades all of that for a cheaper monthly bill.

I'll say it lightly, but I mean it: I believe people are made in the image of a creative God. Creativity isn't a feature we bolt onto our team. It's built into the people on it.

So that's how we run things. When a shoot needs models, we book real ones through modeling agencies and pay them. We never ask them to work for free. When a client needs photos and video, our own team picks up the cameras. When we've grown, we've added people, not just software seats.

Here's where I'll take a shot at my own industry. Some agencies are swapping their creative teams for prompts and charging the exact same retainer. Their clients don't know, and the work slowly starts to sound like everyone else's. If you're paying for creativity, you deserve to know a human is doing it.

## Two superpowers, used for different jobs

The mistake most people make is asking AI and people to do the same job. They each have a superpower. The trick is knowing which one to reach for.

{% table %}
- 
- AI's superpower
- The human superpower
---
- **What it's great at**
- Speed. Research, first concepts, and execution in minutes instead of days
- Ingenious creativity. Understanding how people tick, and making something they feel
---
- **Where we use it**
- Scanning what every competitor is running, gathering what customers are asking, drafting options, formatting, resizing, building
- Finding the real insight, writing the hook, directing the shoot, deciding what's true to the brand
---
- **What it knows**
- What has already been done
- What hasn't been done yet
---
- **Its blind spot**
- Writes to the average, so the work sounds like everyone else
- Slow and expensive when it's stuck on busywork
{% /table %}

AI's superpower is speed. It's the fastest research assistant I've ever had. In an afternoon we can see what a client's competitors are saying, what questions their customers keep asking, and where the gaps are. It helps us sketch ten concepts before lunch. And once the creative is decided, it helps us carry it out quickly, so our team can stay in creative mode longer and serve more clients without cutting corners.

The human superpower is ingenuity. People understand people. They notice the hesitation in a customer's voice, the joke that lands in San Diego but not in Ohio, the thing a founder says offhand that turns out to be the whole campaign.

Here's a real example. [Voyage Air](https://revvia.com/journal/the-brand-that-refused-to-be-a-blip-on-shark-tank) makes guitars that fold in half so you can travel with them. Any AI can tell you the selling point is the fold. A person sitting across from guitar players heard something else: players assume a gimmick has to cost you tone. So the idea became "Hear It to Believe It." Film a real musician choosing a Voyage Air out of a rack of other guitars the same way they'd choose any guitar, because of the sound.

{% figure
   src="/src/assets/journal/human-creativity-age-of-ai/DSC01962.jpg"
   align="left" /%}

Then there was the "fail reel," a concept built around those painful videos of guitars getting wrecked on flights. Jamie, their COO, didn't want the ads to punch down at the baggage crews who handle the cases. So we said "airlines" instead. No machine would have caught that. A good person did.

**AI is the fastest research assistant I've ever had. It's a terrible judge of what makes a person stop scrolling.**

## What happened when we let AI run the creative

We've tested it. When we let pure AI output carry the creative and the copy, something fascinating happened. We often got more leads. And those leads didn't turn into customers.

I want to be clear about what this is. It isn't a peer-reviewed study. It's what we've seen in our own accounts, and it's anecdotal. But it's held up enough times that it changed how we work.

Here's my best explanation of why.

**AI writes to the middle.** It's trained on what most people say, so it produces what most people would say. Ads written that way are easy to agree with and easy to click. They're also easy to forget. You get the curious, not the committed.

**Generic copy doesn't filter anyone out.** Good human copy has a point of view. It's specific about who the product is for, which quietly tells everyone else it isn't for them. That costs you some leads. It also means the people who do raise their hand actually meant it.

**People can smell sameness.** Even when they can't say why, a customer can feel when an ad sounds like the last five they scrolled past. Trust drops a little, and trust is what turns a lead into a sale.

{% figure
   src="/src/assets/journal/human-creativity-age-of-ai/1 (1).png"
   align="right" /%}

If you read the [first Founder's Notes](https://revvia.com/journal/revenue-over-impressions), you know where I'm going with this. A pile of cheap leads is just impressions wearing a nicer outfit. More leads that don't buy aren't a win. They're a bigger to-do list for your sales team.

We care about [revenue over impressions](https://revvia.com/journal/revenue-over-impressions), so we care about customers over leads. And the thing that keeps turning leads into customers, in our experience, is a human being on the creative.

## How the two work together at Revvia

In practice, a project at Revvia moves like a relay. AI runs the legs that are about speed. People run the legs that are about judgment.

1. **AI does the research sweep.** What competitors are running, what customers are searching and asking, and how AI search tools describe the category right now.
1. **People find the insight.** We sit with the founder, talk to customers, walk the shop floor. Our [Built in San Diego](https://revvia.com/journal/built-in-san-diego) interviews taught me the best line in a campaign is usually something the owner said without thinking.
1. **People make the creative.** Our team writes the hooks, plans the story, and shoots the photo and video in-house, with real talent.
1. **AI helps us execute fast.** Variations, resizing, formatting, technical setup, and first drafts we then rewrite. This is the part that buys our team more creative hours.
1. **A human approves everything.** Nothing goes out without a person on our team reviewing it, and every piece goes through the client's own approval before it's published.

{% figure
   src="/src/assets/journal/human-creativity-age-of-ai/Oct. 23rd.png"
   align="left" /%}

And a few lines we don't cross:

- No AI-generated reviews or testimonials. Ever. Only real customers saying real things.
- No AI "models" standing in for paid talent.
- No publishing AI output that a person hasn't read, checked, and owned.

The point of AI at Revvia isn't to make fewer things with fewer people. It's to protect the hours our people spend being creative, so we can give that creativity to more founders.

## The bedtime story test

With five kids, bedtime stories are a nightly event at our house. I could ask AI for a perfect one in about four seconds. Good structure, clean ending, a moral at the bottom.

My kids don't want that one. They want Dad's, the one I make up on the spot, where the dragon is afraid of seagulls and the plot falls apart halfway through. It's objectively worse. They love it anyway, because it's mine and it's made for them.

Your customers are a lot like my kids. They can tell when something was made for them by someone who gets them. That's not a soft, feel-good point. In our experience, it's the difference between a lead and a customer.

So if you're a founder trying to figure out what to do with AI, here's my advice:

- Use it for speed: research, first drafts, and the busywork that eats your week.
- Keep people on the parts that need taste, empathy, and nerve.
- Judge it all by customers and revenue, not by how much you produced.

**Use the machine for speed. Keep the people for the magic.**

If you want a partner who works that way, Revvia is a founder-led growth studio in Carlsbad, and I'd love to talk.

God Bless,

Tim Holt - Revvia Marketing

## Frequently asked questions

### How does Revvia use AI in its marketing work?

Revvia uses AI for speed: competitor and customer research, early concepts, and fast execution such as variations, formatting, and technical setup. People handle the insight, the creative, and the final judgment, and a human reviews every piece before it reaches the client for approval.

### Can AI replace human creativity in marketing?

AI can produce marketing quickly, but it tends to write toward the average of what it has seen. Human creativity brings original ideas, an understanding of what makes people act, and judgment about what fits a brand. Revvia's view is that the best results come from AI supporting people, not replacing them.

### Do AI-generated ads get more leads?

In Revvia's own testing, pure AI-generated creative often produced more leads, but those leads converted to customers less often. This is Revvia's anecdotal experience, not a formal study. Specific, human-written creative tends to attract fewer but more serious buyers.

### Why would AI-written ads bring in lower-quality leads?

Generic copy is easy to click and doesn't screen anyone out, so it draws curious people rather than ready buyers. Human-written ads usually take a clearer point of view about who the product is for, which filters the audience before the click.

### Does Revvia use AI-generated models, images, or reviews?

No. Revvia books real models through modeling agencies and pays them, produces photo and video in-house, and never creates AI-generated reviews or testimonials.

### Where is Revvia Marketing located?

Revvia Marketing is a founder-led growth studio in Carlsbad, California, serving businesses across San Diego County with fractional CMO leadership, paid ads, SEO and AI search visibility, social media, branding, and in-house creative production. Clients work with Revvia month to month.
